THINKING USED We need Glasgow-Blatchford Bleeding score (GBS).  
Variables: blood urea nitrogen, hemoglobin, systolic BP, heart rate, presentation with melena, syncope, hepatic disease, cardiac failure.  
For male: BUN 18 mg/dL, hemoglobin 13 g/dL, systolic BP 130, HR 88, melena present.  
No syncope, no hepatic disease, no heart failure.  
GBS formula: BUN > 30 = 3, 20-30 = 2, 10-20 = 1, <10 = 0.  
Hemoglobin: <12 = 3, 12-13.9 = 2, 14-15.9 = 1, >=16 = 0.  
SBP: <90 = 12, 90-100 = 4, 101-110 = 2, 111-120 = 1, >120 = 0.  
HR: >100 = 13, 90-100 = 7, 80-89 = 3, 70-79 = 1, <70 = 0.  
Melena = 2. Syncope = 2. Hepatic disease = 2. Cardiac failure = 2.  
So compute: BUN 18 => 1. Hemoglobin 13 => 2. SBP 130 => 0. HR 88 => 3. Melena 2. Others 0.
